Title: Nick and Me
Credit: Written by
Author: Author
Source: Adapted from book
Draft date: 7/30/2026

FADE IN:

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - DAY

Period live-action. A worn fifth-floor Milwaukee apartment in the late 1990s. THE NARRATOR, a slight working-class man nearing thirty with short brown hair, carries two grocery bags upstairs.

NICK carries six. He is taller and powerfully built, with unruly reddish-brown hair, a scar on his chin and another over his left eyebrow.

NARRATOR (V.O.)

Big brother was mine. Nick was the guy everybody was scared of. When he walked in the bar, or the gym, people took notice, and man, did they move out of the way.

Inside, OLIVIA “MA” OLSON, a frail woman in her sixties with tired brown eyes and graying brown hair, sits before a game show.

Nick sets down the bags and studies her.

NICK

What’s Ma doin’ back in the bedroom at this time of day?

NARRATOR

I don’t know. Why?

NICK

You got to keep an eye on her. She shouldn’t be sleeping in the middle of the day like that.

Nick points at his brother.

NICK

You got to make her get up. Take her to the store or something.

The Narrator lowers his eyes.

NARRATOR (V.O.)

Nick was right. Let’s face it, Nick became my dad when I was eight.

EXT. KIRK STREET ALLEY - DAY

Years earlier. YOUNG NARRATOR, eight, and YOUNG NICK, twelve, play kickball with neighborhood children. Dented garbage-can lids serve as bases.

MARK CONNER kicks the ball through MRS. ENGEL’S kitchen window.

Glass crashes. MRS. ENGEL storms into the alley.

MRS. ENGEL

Who did that?

Mark freezes. Young Nick steps forward.

YOUNG NICK

I did.

NARRATOR (V.O.)

Ma had to pay for the window. Somehow Nick gave her sixty bucks about a week later. He never told her it was really Mark.

Young Nick puts an arm around his little brother.

YOUNG NICK

I’ll always take care of you. Don’t forget that.

YOUNG NARRATOR

I won’t, Nick.

EXT. PIZZA PARLOR - DAY

The Narrator locks his bicycle beside a neighborhood pizza parlor.

SIONNA MCALLISTER, a slim, pale woman nearing thirty with short brown hair, striking light-blue eyes, jeans, blue tennis shoes and a fish pendant, trips on the pavement and brushes his shoulder.

NARRATOR

Are you okay?

SIONNA

Yes.

She brushes off her black V-neck shirt.

NARRATOR

I work here. I’m just going to work. What is your name?

SIONNA

Sionna.

NARRATOR

Sionna? That’s different.

SIONNA

My parents named me after an Irish goddess. The River Shannon.

They hold each other’s gaze.

NARRATOR

Do you want to come in and have some pizza?

SIONNA

No thanks. I don’t eat pizza. But if you want to go have a cup of coffee sometime, you can call me.

She writes her number. As she walks away, she looks back over her shoulder.

NARRATOR (V.O.)

It was like I knew her. Like I had seen her somewhere before.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - NIGHT

Medication bottles stand in a careful line across the kitchen counter. Ma searches frantically around them.

MA

I can’t find my pain pills. Did we go to church today? Did you see Mrs. Canasta there?

NARRATOR

Ma, we haven’t been to church for over fifteen years. Your pills are right in front of you.

Ma looks at him with glassy, empty eyes.

MA

Oh, right. I am so sorry. I don’t know what got into me.

Later, Ma sleeps in her chair. The Narrator quietly reads Wuthering Heights beneath a lamp.

NARRATOR (V.O.)

I do like reading. I don’t tell nobody that.

He picks up Sionna’s number and dials.

INT. JAVA HUT - DAY

Sionna waits at a rear table in a loose green top. Books protrude from her backpack.

The Narrator sits opposite her, clutching Wuthering Heights.

SIONNA

What are you reading right now?

NARRATOR

Wuthering Heights. It’s the story of this guy and girl and how they grew apart but were really in love with each other.

SIONNA

The love Heathcliff has for Catherine never changes, but in the end that kind of love destroys him. A love that never changes will destroy you.

The Narrator absorbs this.

NARRATOR (V.O.)

Something inside of me was coming awake.

Sionna opens another book, showing him a passage on Buddhism.

SIONNA

The cycle of life is ever-changing. We have to be prepared to change with it, to let go of things.

NARRATOR

Nobody ever wanted to talk to me about this stuff before.

SIONNA

You matter.

Their hands touch across the table.

INT. YOGA STUDIO - DAY

The Narrator struggles on a purple mat among practiced students. His T-shirt falls over his face during downward dog.

Sionna suppresses a laugh.

Later, everyone lies still in corpse pose. The Narrator’s breathing slows.

Outside the studio, he looks astonished.

SIONNA

You were really great at that.

NARRATOR

Are you kidding me?

SIONNA

You caught on quickly. You did like it, didn’t you?

NARRATOR

Yeah. I did like it. I just felt so dumb.

SIONNA

That’s why they call it practice.

They smile.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - NIGHT

A blue weekly pill organizer rests beside Ma. College forms and textbooks cover the table.

NARRATOR

I’m going to community college. I’m getting a degree in business.

Nick stares at him.

NICK

How the hell are you going to pay for that? How are you going to have time to watch Ma?

NARRATOR

I applied for a student loan. This was my idea.

NICK

You’re letting this chick tell you what to do.

NARRATOR

It would mean a lot if you could just be on my side for once. You are my brother.

Nick rises over him.

NICK

Do you remember your own life? How many times have I saved your ass?

NARRATOR

I know, Nick.

Nick lightly punches his arm.

NICK

You’re not just my brother. You’re my best friend. Nothing is ever gonna come between us. Not school, not Ma, and not this girl either.

The Narrator forces a smile.

INT. JOE’S BAR AND GRILL - NIGHT

Smoke hangs above a crowded pool room. NICK, BOB and SAMMY drink beside the Narrator.

Across the room, Nick’s former girlfriend LINDSEY stands with MARCO, her dark-haired new boyfriend.

Nick pushes through the crowd.

NICK

Who’s this?

LINDSEY

Just an old friend.

MARCO puts an arm around her.

MARCO

You got a problem with me?

Nick’s fists clench.

NARRATOR

Nick, leave it alone. Lindsey can see whoever she wants.

NICK

Keep out of it.

NARRATOR

No one cares about your ego, Nick!

Nick draws back his fist. The Narrator flinches but holds his ground.

JOE and BOUNCER HARRIS seize Nick. Nick head-butts Harris as Lindsey pulls Marco toward the exit.

The Narrator runs.

EXT. MILWAUKEE SIDEWALK - NIGHT

He sprints through the cold, gasping, until he reaches Sionna’s duplex.

INT. SIONNA’S LIVING ROOM - NIGHT

The Narrator lies with his head in Sionna’s lap as she strokes his hair.

SIONNA

Your brother tried to hit you.

NARRATOR

He does care about me. I can’t explain Nick completely.

SIONNA

You are showing him that you don’t need him. You can’t let him intimidate you or stop you from your purpose.

NARRATOR

What is my purpose?

SIONNA

To find the true self within you, and to honor it. To be the best person you can while you’re here.

NARRATOR

The first time I met you, it was like I had finally come home.

SIONNA

Me too, honey. Me too.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - NIGHT

Sionna serves Ma warm peach pie. Ma laughs as she tells a childhood story.

Nick enters in a brown suede jacket and heavy boots. He sees Sionna for the first time.

SIONNA

You must be Nick. Nice to meet you.

Nick reluctantly shakes her hand.

NICK

I hear you’re a university lady.

SIONNA

That’s right.

NICK

Sometimes all that school just doesn’t pay off.

SIONNA

There are more females in college today than males. Diversity helps us learn. After all, we are all humans trying to live on this planet and make a difference.

She carries Ma’s plate into the kitchen.

Nick stares after her, silenced.

Before leaving, he pulls the Narrator close by his shirt.

NICK

Take care of Ma.

NARRATOR

You know I will.

EXT. WERNER AND LOWE INTERSECTION - NIGHT

Nick’s burnt-orange 1980 Pontiac Trans Am, yellow flames along its sides and an eagle on its hood, speeds through the intersection.

Inside, Nick’s eyes are dulled by drink. Ma sits beside him in her church coat.

Headlights surge from the passenger side.

Metal explodes. The Trans Am spins across the wet pavement.

INT. POLICE STATION - DAY

OFFICER MIKE DUPUIS, a broad man with graying hair and a thin mustache, sits opposite the Narrator.

OFFICER DUPUIS

There has been a serious accident involving your brother and your mother. Your brother is in serious condition.

The Narrator grips a paper cup.

OFFICER DUPUIS

Your mother passed away at the scene.

The cup buckles in the Narrator’s hand.

INT. HOSPITAL ICU - DAY

Through a window, Nick lies unconscious among tubes and machines, his face bruised and bandaged.

A nurse waits beside the Narrator.

NURSE PATRICIA OWENS

Would you like to see him?

NARRATOR

No.

He turns away.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - NIGHT

Ma’s chair is empty. Her afghan lies folded across one arm.

The Narrator collapses beside it.

NARRATOR

He killed her. He fucking killed my mom.

Sionna kneels and holds him as he sobs.

INT. SIONNA’S LIVING ROOM - NIGHT

Months later. Sionna opens her front door to find Nick standing outside. His body has weakened since the crash, but his scarred face burns with rage.

NICK

Can I talk to you?

SIONNA

Definitely. Have a seat.

NICK

You’re the reason I’m probably going to jail.

SIONNA

What makes you think I’m responsible?

Nick drives her backward against the hallway wall.

NICK

You confused his stupid brain with school and other shit. If it wasn’t for you, Ma would be alive.

SIONNA

Nick, calm down. This has nothing to do with me.

Nick grips her throat and opens a switchblade.

NICK

Break it off with him tonight. I’ll be back in one week, and I better see some cash.

The front door bangs open. The Narrator rushes in.

NARRATOR

Holy shit, Nick! What are you doing?

He grabs Nick’s arm.

The knife plunges beneath Sionna’s left collarbone.

She gasps. Blood spreads across her shirt.

Nick elbows his brother away and runs.

The Narrator catches Sionna as she slides down the wall.

NARRATOR

Sionna. Stay with me.

He tears away her shirt and presses both hands over the wound.

NARRATOR

Hurry up. Hurry up.

Sirens approach. Sionna’s breath turns shallow beneath his bloody hands.

INT. SIONNA’S LIVING ROOM - DAY

Months later. Sionna sits rigidly in a chair, her left arm supported after surgery. The bright life has gone from her blue eyes.

NARRATOR

You know I love you, right?

SIONNA

I can’t feel me. I can’t feel myself anymore.

He kneels before her.

NARRATOR

You are safe. I will protect you with my life.

SIONNA

As long as there is any doubt, I can’t go on with you. I need to go to Mom and Dad’s for a while.

NARRATOR

Please stay. Stay here with me.

She looks down at her hands. He understands.

EXT. SEATTLE STREET - DAY

Seven years later. Rain mists over downtown Seattle.

The Narrator, now older, stronger and dressed as a successful businessman, walks past a bright sign: TONY’S BOY’S PIZZA.

NARRATOR (V.O.)

Thanks to my old boss, Tony, I had done pretty well for myself. I now had my own chain of pizzerias.

He passes a woman with long brown hair and stops, remembering Sionna.

NARRATOR (V.O.)

Seven years of change, seven years of loneliness, and seven years of hatred for my brother.

INT. YOGA STUDIO - NIGHT

Candles flicker around the Narrator as he meditates.

His closed eyes soften.

NARRATOR (V.O.)

Holding onto resentment kept me in my own prison. I finally had the key to unlock it, and it was inside myself.

His eyes open.

INT. GREEN BAY CORRECTIONAL INSTITUTE VISITING ROOM - DAY

Nick enters in prison clothes. He is thinner, gray at the temples, his eyes burdened by years behind bars.

The brothers sit on opposite sides of thick glass and lift yellow telephones.

NICK

Hi, Bro. Glad you came.

NARRATOR

I’m here to tell you some things. I would appreciate it if you would just listen.

Nick nods.

NARRATOR

You killed my mother in a drunk-driving accident. You held me down my entire life. And you tried to kill the love of my life, which drove her away.

Nick looks down.

NARRATOR

But this is the most important bit. I forgive you.

Nick looks up.

NARRATOR

I forgive you because it sets me free. It releases your control over me forever.

Tears stream down Nick’s scarred face.

NICK

I’m so sorry. You’re right. I did all that shit. You’re a big man, and I’m a small one.

A guard signals that time is nearly over.

NARRATOR

I want you to get better. After all is said and done, I still love you for being my brother.

NICK

What are you doing now?

NARRATOR

I own a pizza chain in Seattle. You always said I couldn’t do it. I did it, Nick.

Nick smiles through his tears.

NICK

President Olson. That’s fucking amazing. I am so proud of you, man. I love you.

NARRATOR

I love you too.

Nick is led away. The Narrator hangs up the telephone and exhales thirty years of fear.

INT. MILWAUKEE AIRPORT - DAY

The Narrator approaches the boarding gate. Sunlight breaks through the terminal windows.

The attendant scans his boarding pass.

INSERT - BOARDING PASS

PETER OLSON.

The Narrator smiles.

NARRATOR

That’s me. President Peter Olson.

He walks toward the plane without looking back.

FADE OUT.

THE END
